insert into public.flashcards (id, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('f4-001','core',4,'4.1',$$What is a secure baseline?$$,$$A documented, approved standard configuration for a system type; establish it, deploy it, and maintain it (audit for drift, update with patches).$$),
('f4-002','core',4,'4.1',$$Name five host hardening techniques.$$,$$Remove unnecessary software/services, close unused ports, change default passwords, apply patches, enable host firewall + endpoint protection, enforce disk encryption.$$),
('f4-003','core',4,'4.1',$$Why is changing default credentials critical?$$,$$Default usernames/passwords are published and scanned for automatically - the most common IoT/appliance compromise vector.$$),
('f4-004','core',4,'4.1',$$Wireless site survey and heat map - purpose?$$,$$Survey measures RF coverage/interference to place APs; heat map visualizes signal strength - controls coverage spillage outside the facility.$$),
('f4-005','core',4,'4.1',$$WPA3 improvements over WPA2?$$,$$SAE handshake (resists offline dictionary attacks), forward secrecy, stronger encryption, protected management frames.$$),
('f4-006','core',4,'4.1',$$Enterprise (802.1X) vs Personal (PSK/SAE) Wi-Fi authentication?$$,$$Personal: one shared passphrase. Enterprise: per-user credentials via RADIUS (individual accountability, revocable per user).$$),
('f4-007','core',4,'4.1',$$What does RADIUS do in wireless security?$$,$$Central authentication server for 802.1X - validates user credentials and returns access decisions to the AP/switch.$$),
('f4-008','core',4,'4.1',$$BYOD vs COPE vs CYOD?$$,$$BYOD: employee-owned (privacy/control tension). COPE: corporate-owned, personal use allowed. CYOD: employee picks from approved corporate list.$$),
('f4-009','core',4,'4.1',$$Key MDM capabilities?$$,$$Enforce passcode/encryption, remote wipe, app management, containerization (work/personal separation), compliance/posture reporting.$$),
('f4-010','core',4,'4.1',$$Cellular vs Wi-Fi vs Bluetooth - security notes for mobile?$$,$$Cellular: carrier-encrypted but not org-visible. Wi-Fi: rogue AP/evil twin risk - use VPN. Bluetooth: keep non-discoverable, patch (bluesnarfing/bluejacking legacy attacks).$$),
('f4-011','core',4,'4.2',$$Asset management: why does security depend on it?$$,$$You cannot protect what you do not know exists - inventory drives patching, monitoring, and incident scoping. Includes assignment/ownership, classification.$$),
('f4-012','core',4,'4.2',$$Secure asset disposal/decommissioning steps?$$,$$Sanitize (wipe/crypto-erase), destroy if required (shred/degauss), certify destruction, update inventory - data retention rules decide what must be preserved first.$$),
('f4-013','core',4,'4.2',$$Sanitization: wiping vs degaussing vs physical destruction?$$,$$Wiping: overwrite data (reusable disk). Degaussing: magnetic erasure (kills drive electronics; magnetic media only). Destruction: shredding/incineration - highest assurance.$$),
('f4-014','core',4,'4.3',$$Vulnerability management lifecycle?$$,$$Identify (scan/feeds) -> analyze (confirm, prioritize by CVSS/exposure) -> remediate (patch, compensate) -> validate (rescan) -> report.$$),
('f4-015','core',4,'4.3',$$Credentialed vs non-credentialed scans?$$,$$Credentialed: logs into targets - deep, accurate (missing patches, configs). Non-credentialed: outside view - what an attacker sees, more false positives.$$),
('f4-016','core',4,'4.3',$$False positive vs false negative in scanning?$$,$$False positive: reported flaw that does not exist (wastes time). False negative: real flaw not reported (dangerous - unseen risk).$$),
('f4-017','core',4,'4.3',$$What is CVSS and its rough severity bands?$$,$$Common Vulnerability Scoring System 0-10: Low 0.1-3.9, Medium 4.0-6.9, High 7.0-8.9, Critical 9.0-10 - prioritization input, not the whole story (add exposure/exploitability).$$),
('f4-018','core',4,'4.3',$$CVE vs CVSS?$$,$$CVE: the ID/catalog entry for a specific vulnerability. CVSS: the severity score assigned to it.$$),
('f4-019','core',4,'4.3',$$Vulnerability response options (exam list)?$$,$$Patch, insurance (transfer), segmentation, compensating controls, exceptions/exemptions (documented risk acceptance).$$),
('f4-020','core',4,'4.3',$$What is a bug bounty program?$$,$$Paying external researchers for responsibly disclosed vulnerabilities - crowdsourced testing under defined rules.$$),
('f4-021','core',4,'4.3',$$Penetration test vs vulnerability scan?$$,$$Scan: automated identification of potential flaws. Pen test: humans actively EXPLOIT flaws to demonstrate real impact and chained attacks.$$),
('f4-022','core',4,'4.3',$$Static (SAST) vs dynamic (DAST) application analysis?$$,$$SAST: analyze source/binary without running (early, whole codebase, false positives). DAST: test the running app (real behavior, later in cycle).$$),
('f4-023','core',4,'4.3',$$What is threat feed / OSINT in vulnerability identification?$$,$$External intelligence sources (open-source intel, vendor advisories, dark web monitoring, info-sharing organizations) informing what to look for and prioritize.$$),
('f4-024','core',4,'4.4',$$What does a SIEM do (four functions)?$$,$$Aggregate logs from everywhere, normalize, correlate events across sources, alert - plus dashboards, retention for investigations/compliance.$$),
('f4-025','core',4,'4.4',$$Log aggregation vs correlation?$$,$$Aggregation: collecting logs centrally. Correlation: linking related events across sources into one meaningful alert (failed VPN logins + new admin account = incident).$$),
('f4-026','core',4,'4.4',$$What is SCAP used for?$$,$$Automating configuration compliance and vulnerability checking against standards (benchmarks) using standardized formats (OVAL, XCCDF, CVE, CPE).$$),
('f4-027','core',4,'4.4',$$Monitoring activities (exam list)?$$,$$Log aggregation, alerting, scanning, reporting, archiving, alert response and remediation/validation (quarantine, alert tuning).$$),
('f4-028','core',4,'4.4',$$What is alert tuning and why?$$,$$Adjusting detection thresholds/rules to cut false positives - alert fatigue causes real incidents to be missed.$$),
('f4-029','core',4,'4.4',$$SNMP traps vs polling?$$,$$Polling: manager queries devices on schedule. Traps: device pushes an alert immediately when something happens - use SNMPv3 for auth+encryption.$$),
('f4-030','core',4,'4.4',$$NetFlow vs full packet capture?$$,$$NetFlow: metadata about conversations (cheap, scalable, no payload). PCAP: full packets (deep forensics, heavy storage). Use flow broadly, capture selectively.$$),
('f4-031','core',4,'4.4',$$What is DLP and its enforcement points?$$,$$Data Loss Prevention: detects/blocks sensitive data leaving via endpoints (USB/clipboard), email, web uploads, cloud - based on content rules/classification.$$),
('f4-032','core',4,'4.4',$$What is a benchmark (e.g., CIS) in monitoring/compliance?$$,$$A published hardening standard to configure and continuously audit systems against (baseline compliance scanning).$$),
('f4-033','core',4,'4.4',$$Antivirus vs EDR?$$,$$AV: signature/heuristic blocking of known malware. EDR: continuous endpoint telemetry, behavioral detection, investigation and response actions (isolate host, kill process, roll back).$$),
('f4-034','core',4,'4.5',$$Firewall rule best practice: implicit deny?$$,$$Rules permit only required traffic; everything not explicitly allowed is denied by a final implicit deny - review rules regularly.$$),
('f4-035','core',4,'4.5',$$What are screened subnets used for in enterprise capability terms?$$,$$Placing public-facing services in a buffer zone with firewall policy on both sides - contain compromise of exposed services.$$),
('f4-036','core',4,'4.5',$$IDS/IPS signature vs anomaly/behavioral detection?$$,$$Signature: match known patterns (precise, misses novel attacks). Anomaly: flag deviation from learned baseline (catches new attacks, more false positives).$$),
('f4-037','core',4,'4.5',$$Web filtering: agent-based vs centralized proxy; reputation vs category?$$,$$Agent: filter on the device (works anywhere). Proxy: central chokepoint. Filtering by URL category (gambling/malware) and reputation scores; block lists + DNS filtering.$$),
('f4-038','core',4,'4.5',$$What is Group Policy (GPO) used for in security?$$,$$Centrally enforcing OS security settings across Windows domains (password policy, lockout, restrictions, script execution).$$),
('f4-039','core',4,'4.5',$$What is SELinux (exam view)?$$,$$Mandatory access control enforcement in Linux - policies constrain what each process may do regardless of user permissions.$$),
('f4-040','core',4,'4.5',$$Secure protocol swaps: name the secure version and port - Telnet, FTP, HTTP, SMTP submission, IMAP, POP3, LDAP, DNS?$$,$$SSH 22, SFTP 22 (or FTPS 990), HTTPS 443, SMTPS/submission with STARTTLS 587, IMAPS 993, POP3S 995, LDAPS 636, DNSSEC/DoH (853 DoT, 443 DoH).$$),
('f4-041','core',4,'4.5',$$Email security: what do SPF, DKIM, DMARC each verify?$$,$$SPF: sending server is authorized for the domain. DKIM: message signed by the domain, unaltered. DMARC: policy for handling SPF/DKIM failures + reporting.$$),
('f4-042','core',4,'4.5',$$What is an email gateway?$$,$$A mail relay filtering inbound/outbound email: spam, malware, phishing, DLP - first line against message-based vectors.$$),
('f4-043','core',4,'4.5',$$What is file integrity monitoring (FIM)?$$,$$Detecting changes to critical files/configs by comparing hashes against a known-good baseline; alerts on tampering (e.g., Tripwire).$$),
('f4-044','core',4,'4.5',$$NAC: what does posture checking add?$$,$$Beyond identity, NAC verifies device health (patched, AV running, encrypted) before admitting; non-compliant devices go to remediation VLAN.$$),
('f4-045','core',4,'4.5',$$What is DNS filtering as a protective capability?$$,$$Blocking resolution of known-malicious domains - cheap, broad protection against phishing/C2 callbacks.$$),
('f4-046','core',4,'4.6',$$Identity lifecycle: provisioning to de-provisioning?$$,$$Create account with least-privilege rights on hire/role change; disable/remove promptly at exit - orphaned accounts are attacker gold.$$),
('f4-047','core',4,'4.6',$$What is federation?$$,$$Trusting an external identity provider to authenticate users across organizations (login with corporate/Google identity) via SAML/OIDC.$$),
('f4-048','core',4,'4.6',$$SSO risks and mitigations?$$,$$One credential unlocks everything - protect with MFA, conditional access, session monitoring; benefit: fewer passwords, central revocation.$$),
('f4-049','core',4,'4.6',$$How does SAML flow work (IdP/SP)?$$,$$User hits Service Provider -> redirected to Identity Provider -> authenticates -> IdP issues signed assertion -> SP grants access. Passwords never touch the SP.$$),
('f4-050','core',4,'4.6',$$OAuth vs SAML vs OIDC in one line each?$$,$$OAuth 2.0: delegated AUTHORIZATION (app acts on your behalf). SAML: XML-based enterprise SSO authentication. OIDC: authentication layer on OAuth (modern login).$$),
('f4-051','core',4,'4.6',$$Interoperability and attestation in IAM (exam terms)?$$,$$Interoperability: identity systems working across platforms via standards. Attestation: periodic review certifying users' access is still appropriate.$$),
('f4-052','core',4,'4.6',$$The three (plus) authentication factor categories?$$,$$Something you KNOW (password/PIN), HAVE (token, phone, smart card), ARE (biometrics); plus somewhere you are (location) and something you do (behavior).$$),
('f4-053','core',4,'4.6',$$Is password + security question MFA?$$,$$No - both are "something you know." MFA requires two DIFFERENT factor categories.$$),
('f4-054','core',4,'4.6',$$Hard vs soft authentication tokens?$$,$$Hard: physical device (key fob, smart card, security key). Soft: app-based (authenticator app TOTP, push notification).$$),
('f4-055','core',4,'4.6',$$Password best practices per exam (length/complexity/age/reuse)?$$,$$Length beats complexity; avoid forced frequent expiration without cause; block reuse; check against known-breached lists; consider passphrases and managers.$$),
('f4-056','core',4,'4.6',$$What is a password vault/manager?$$,$$Encrypted store generating and filling unique strong passwords per site - eliminates reuse, protects against credential stuffing.$$),
('f4-057','core',4,'4.6',$$What is passwordless authentication?$$,$$Replacing passwords with cryptographic credentials (passkeys/FIDO2 security keys, biometric-unlocked device keys) - phishing-resistant.$$),
('f4-058','core',4,'4.6',$$What does PAM (privileged access management) provide?$$,$$Vaulted admin credentials, just-in-time elevation, ephemeral credentials, session recording - controls and audits the most dangerous accounts.$$),
('f4-059','core',4,'4.6',$$Just-in-time permissions and ephemeral credentials?$$,$$Grant admin rights only when needed, expiring automatically; credentials generated per-session so nothing standing exists to steal.$$),
('f4-060','core',4,'4.7',$$Benefits of security automation (exam list)?$$,$$Efficiency/time saving, enforcing baselines, standard infra configs, scaling securely, employee retention (less toil), faster reaction time, workforce multiplier.$$),
('f4-061','core',4,'4.7',$$Automation use cases (exam list)?$$,$$User/resource provisioning, guard rails, security groups, ticket creation/escalation, disabling access, continuous integration/testing, API integrations.$$),
('f4-062','core',4,'4.7',$$Considerations/risks of automation?$$,$$Complexity, cost, single point of failure, technical debt, ongoing supportability - a broken playbook fails at machine speed.$$),
('f4-063','core',4,'4.7',$$What is SOAR vs SIEM?$$,$$SIEM detects and alerts; SOAR acts - orchestrates tools and runs automated playbooks (enrich, contain, notify) in response.$$),
('f4-064','core',4,'4.8',$$The incident response lifecycle (order!)?$$,$$Preparation -> Detection -> Analysis -> Containment -> Eradication -> Recovery -> Lessons Learned.$$),
('f4-065','core',4,'4.8',$$Containment vs eradication vs recovery?$$,$$Containment: stop the spread (isolate hosts). Eradication: remove the cause (malware, accounts, vulnerabilities). Recovery: restore normal operations safely.$$),
('f4-066','core',4,'4.8',$$What is a lessons-learned/after-action review?$$,$$Post-incident meeting documenting what happened, what worked/failed, and concrete improvements - closes the loop into Preparation.$$),
('f4-067','core',4,'4.8',$$Tabletop exercise vs simulation in IR training/testing?$$,$$Tabletop: talk through a scenario around a table. Simulation: hands-on drill (e.g., phishing simulation, mock incident) exercising real procedures.$$),
('f4-068','core',4,'4.8',$$What is root cause analysis?$$,$$Determining the underlying cause (not just symptoms) of an incident so the fix prevents recurrence.$$),
('f4-069','core',4,'4.8',$$What is threat hunting?$$,$$Proactively searching for signs of compromise that evaded detection, hypothesis-driven, using telemetry and threat intel.$$),
('f4-070','core',4,'4.8',$$Digital forensics: order of the process?$$,$$Identify -> Preserve (image, write-blockers) -> Collect -> Analyze -> Report; maintain chain of custody throughout.$$),
('f4-071','core',4,'4.8',$$What is chain of custody?$$,$$Documented, unbroken record of who handled evidence, when, and how - preserves admissibility and integrity.$$),
('f4-072','core',4,'4.8',$$What is legal hold / e-discovery?$$,$$Legal hold: preservation order suspending normal deletion for potentially relevant data. E-discovery: producing electronic evidence for legal proceedings.$$),
('f4-073','core',4,'4.8',$$Order of volatility - most to least volatile?$$,$$CPU registers/cache -> RAM -> swap/temp -> disk -> remote logs -> backups/archival media. Collect the most volatile first.$$),
('f4-074','core',4,'4.9',$$Name six log data sources used in investigations.$$,$$Firewall logs, application logs, endpoint/EDR logs, OS-specific security logs, IPS/IDS logs, network logs (flow, DNS, DHCP), plus metadata, packet captures, vulnerability scans, dashboards.$$),
('f4-075','core',4,'4.9',$$What can firewall logs tell you?$$,$$Allowed/denied connections: source/destination IPs, ports, timestamps - beaconing, exfiltration attempts, scanning.$$),
('f4-076','core',4,'4.9',$$What is packet capture metadata vs content?$$,$$Metadata: headers - who/when/how much. Content: payloads - what was actually sent (needs decryption for TLS).$$),
('f4-077','core',4,'4.9',$$Windows Event Log types relevant to security?$$,$$Security log (logons 4624/4625, account changes), System, Application; forwarded centrally to the SIEM.$$),
('f4-078','core',4,'4.1',$$What is configuration enforcement?$$,$$Automatically detecting and correcting drift from the secure baseline (via GPO, MDM, config management tools).$$),
('f4-079','core',4,'4.5',$$What is XDR vs EDR?$$,$$EDR is endpoint-only; XDR correlates detections across endpoint, network, email, identity, and cloud into unified investigations.$$),
('f4-080','core',4,'4.5',$$User behavior analytics (UBA/UEBA)?$$,$$Baselines normal per-user/entity behavior and flags anomalies (odd hours, mass downloads, novel systems) - catches insider threats and stolen credentials.$$);
